</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Agent Systems with Memory</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the success of retrieval-augmented-generation (RAG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, existing agent systems have also incorporated an external memory in vector databases to overcome the limitations of limited context in LLMs. Park <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> maintained agent-specific memory to store the experiences of each persona as different agents interacted with each other in a simulation. MemGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> proposed holding two types of external databases: storing raw conversation history in its recall memory and essential information about the user in archival memory. OS-Copilot <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> proposed a more cognitively aligned approach, storing the user and the agent’s action history information in a declarative memory database while storing information about tools in a procedural memory database to support learning new tools for acting as an operating system. Although impressive, these systems only support storing text information in their external memory databases. More recently, JARVIS-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> proposed storing both the plan and images in memory to improve performance at solving tasks in the Minecraft game environment. However, JARVIS-1 maintained a non-hierarchical memory while only supporting image and text modalities, not aligning with the human memory system (<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Alignment with Human Cognition ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>), leading to inefficient retrieval with increased memory size. In contrast to the existing agent systems, our AUGUSTUS maintains the contextual memory as a hierarchical multimodal database, storing semantic concepts at the top level with the associated context stored under the corresponding concepts, reducing the search space.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>System Description</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15261v1/cutegpt_conversation.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/452;" width="476" height="452" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Snapshot of a conversation between a user and AUGUSTUS about the user’s pet.</span> Our system calls different functions corresponding to the four stages of operation to engage with the user while thinking internally <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for careful planning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by the ability of humans to leverage context during social interactions based on experience and knowledge from memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, we present AUGUSTUS, an agent system aligned with the ideas of the human memory system in cognitive science (<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Alignment with Human Cognition ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). As shown in <a href="#S0.F1" title="In AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we design our system with four stages connected in a loop: (i) encode, (ii) store in memory, (iii) retrieve, and (iv) act. Following previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, we also equip our agent system with the ability to think <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> for more accurate tool selection and function calling. We demonstrate a snapshot of AUGUSTUS’ conversation with a user in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> during which our agent tries to understand and store context about the user in the contextual memory and remember the context for providing personalized responses to the user in a future conversation, as shown in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5 Conclusion ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first provide the technical details for our system’s four stages. Then, we explain our process of conceptualizing information as tags for storage in contextual memory (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Storing Concepts in Contextual Memory ‣ 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). Third, we present our Contextual-Personalized (CoPe) search algorithm to efficiently retrieve information about the user (<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Retrieve from Contextual Memory: CoPe search ‣ 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). Lastly, we briefly describe the LLM-generated function calling mechanism orchestrating our system (<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Function Calling ‣ 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Encode.</span> We leverage a set of foundation models to equip AUGUSTUS with the ability to understand multimodal inputs from the user, as shown in <a href="#S2.T1" title="In 2.1 LLM-Driven Agent Systems ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Following the language-of-thought-hypothesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, we encode all information into language form to be manipulated and stored in memory by the LLM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15261v1/cutegpt_insert.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/305;" width="452" height="305" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization of Information in the Contextual Memory.</span> AUGUSTUS conceptualizes information into semantic tags connected with the corresponding context node, creating associations among the tags. <span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the figure</span>, the expanded context node is associated with the concepts listed in its “tags” field. Note that we do not show all associations in the figure due to space constraints (<span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">playful-dog-park</span>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Store in Memory.</span> AUGUSTUS maintains three types of memory:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Context Memory (limited size)</span>: The information in the in-context memory is always visible to the LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. It includes instructions for function-calling, starter information about the user, and the agent’s persona. AUGUSTUS can modify the memory contents about the user and its persona <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall Memory (unlimited size)</span>: We log the complete conversation history in recall memory, an external SQLite database <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, to equip our system to recall essential conversations. AUGUSTUS can retrieve contents from the recall memory using the <span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">conversation_search</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> function autonomously.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contextual Memory (unlimited size)</span>: Our agent system maintains the contextual memory as a hierarchical multimodal database to store and retrieve user context. Any vital user information is first abstracted into semantic tags (concepts) and stored along with the context from the conversation in the contextual memory. Our agent can insert and retrieve information in/from the contextual memory with the <span id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">contextual_memory_insert</span> and <span id="S3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cope_search</span> functions, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieve.</span> Our system’s retrieval is completely autonomous, with the agent deciding when to search for information. AUGUSTUS retrieves information from the recall memory with a paginated search over the storage for the query <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. To retrieve information from the contextual memory, we formulate a two-stage search approach, first searching for relevant tags (semantic abstraction of context) and then searching over the context nodes associated with the retrieved tags to return personalized information relevant to the present conversation (<a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5 Conclusion ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). We term our algorithm <span id="S3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Co</span>ntextual-<span id="S3.p8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pe</span>rsonalized (<span id="S3.p8.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoPe</span>) search.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Act.</span> We equip AUGUSTUS with a range of actions (<a href="#S2.T1" title="In 2.1 LLM-Driven Agent Systems ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), including sending a message, understanding and generating an image, audio, and video, and the ability to edit images. Note that different actions can be chained together, with sending a message to the user always being the last action in the chain.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Storing Concepts in Contextual Memory</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the information inside the contextual memory is organized in a graph structure with associations among tags based on shared context. We store the concept name inside a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tag node</span> and the multimodal user context inside a <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">context node</span>. If two tag nodes share a context node, they must have an edge; if two tag nodes have an edge, they must share at least one context node.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During a conversation, AUGUSTUS initiates the information insertion process in the contextual memory with a <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">contextual_memory_insert</span> function call. The first step is conceptualizing the encoded information into semantic tags. Next, depending on the modality of the information, our system pushes non-text information to cloud storage and stores the corresponding URL, a list of tags, encoded information, modality type, and timestamp in a context node object, as shown at the top in <a href="#S3.F3" title="In 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Note that for text modality, the URL field is null. To ensure the high quality of generated tags, we leverage LLMs’ in-context-learning (ICL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> ability (LLMs) and pass demonstrations for improved conceptualization. We share the demonstrations in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15261v1/cutegpt_cope_search.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/337;" width="476" height="337" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval with CoPe Search.</span> Given a query, AUGUSTUS retrieves relevant concept (<span id="S3.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pet; dog; corgi</span>). followed by concept-driven context retrieval to send a personalized response to the user (<span id="S3.F4.7" class="ltx_text">name and image of the <span id="S3.F4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pet</span> to help user reminisce</span>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Retrieve from Contextual Memory: CoPe search</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cope_search</span> function call, our system first searches over the concepts and then retrieves the concept-relevant context to respond to the user, as shown in <a href="#S3.F4" title="In 3.1 Storing Concepts in Contextual Memory ‣ 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Naturally, the search becomes more compute-intensive as the number of tags grows. Therefore, we construct a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contextual tree</span> of tags to perform an efficient search over many concepts, with each node representing the average of context embeddings associated with the corresponding tag. Owing to the high dimensionality of embeddings, we perform UMAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> over tag features and iteratively cluster tags with HDBSCAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> until there’s a single cluster as the root node. As mentioned in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Storing Concepts in Contextual Memory ‣ 3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, in our contextual memory, we associate concepts based on shared context nodes, much like a <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">personalized (knowledge) graph</span> structurally dependent on user context information from their interactions with AUGUSTUS. We leverage these associations to further boost our concept retrieval performance by learning concept representations about the user context to predict a set of personalized tags. We collectively use the two sets of tags for the context retrieval step, returning the context nodes based on their cosine similarity with the query embeddings. We only compare the similarity values for the concept-relevant context nodes, reducing the search space, unlike searching through all the nodes in a sequential vector database <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We use the same embedding model to compute the embeddings for the tags, query, and multimodal context nodes. We provide our CoPe search logic in <a href="#algorithm1" title="In A.3 Stage-3: Retrieve ‣ Appendix A Alignment with Human Cognition ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Function Calling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The operation of AUGUSTUS is driven by an LLM through a function calling mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We include the description of functions responsible for the four stages in our agent as a part of our system prompt instructions to the LLM. During a conversation, the LLM generates the values for the parameters to be passed into the corresponding function for smooth operation. For example, the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">send_message</span> function expects a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">string</span> (message) data type; therefore, the LLM shall pass a <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">string</span> to the corresponding function call. We list all the supported functions in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Implementation Details ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
