</section>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:144%;">Appendix</span></p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix, we first share details about the alignment of AUGUSTUS’ system design wigh the human cognition system in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Alignment with Human Cognition ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Next, we share the system prompt for AUGUSTUS in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C System Prompt for AUGUSTUS ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. Secondly, we present information about the demonstration examples for accurate conceptualization of information into semantic tags in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Tag Generation with LLM ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. Lastly, we provide details about all supported function calls in our multimodal agent systems in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Implementation Details ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Alignment with Human Cognition</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We observe that the cognitive science community broadly divides the human memory system into three stages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>: (i) <span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">encode</span> sensory inputs; (ii) <span id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">store</span> in memory; and (iii) <span id="A1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">retrieve</span> information. We also formulate AUGUSTUS along similar lines, aligning our system (memory) design with human cognition. Moreover, we term the last stage attributed to the tool execution in AUGUSTUS and action/reaction in humans as <span id="A1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">act</span>. All four stages can be considered connected in a closed loop form, with the <span id="A1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">act</span> stage providing new information to the <span id="A1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">encode</span> stage, as shown in <a href="#S0.F1" title="In AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Stage-1: Encode</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Humans.</span> Encoding information perceived through the sensory organs is the initial step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> in the human memory system. It involves converting the visual, acoustic, tactile, and semantic signals into a representation storable in memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUGUSTUS</span> Our system also encodes the user inputs as the first step. AUGUSTUS can handle inputs across multiple modalities: image and video (visual), audio (acoustic), and text (semantic). We do not support touch (tactile) modality; however, we believe supporting touch modality is straightforward, which we leave for future work. We use modality-specific encoders to encode inputs, following the existing any-to-any works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. As an LLM lies at the core of our system, we convert all non-text inputs into text caption, following the language of thought hypothesis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, for the LLM to reason and make decisions in our system. We provide more details about our encoders in <a href="#S3" title="3 System Description ‣ AUGUSTUS: An LLM-Driven Multimodal Agent System with Contextualized User Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Sec.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Note that, in our contextual memory, we store both the text caption and the corresponding non-text input media to prevent any loss in multimodal context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Stage-2: Store in Memory</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Humans.</span> During the second stage, the encoded information is forwarded to the memory for storage and manipulation. From the perspective of our system design, we focus on two main memory types in humans: (i) <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">working memory</span> has a limited capacity and is responsible for information manipulation and reasoning, along with other tasks that require planning and decision making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>; and (ii) <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">long-term memory</span> is believed to have unlimited storage capacity, storing information indefinitely which needs to be retrieved for use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. We take inspiration from explicit long-term memory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> comprised of semantic and episodic memory, with the former storing information about concepts, mainly linguistic, and the latter storing chronological information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUGUSTUS</span>. With an LLM as our system’s orchestrator, we recognize the similarities between the in-context memory in LLMs and the working memory in humans. Like working memory, in-context memory also has a limited size (context length) in LLMs. Moreover, the prompt passed as the context to the LLM can be used to affect the response behavior of LLMs, imitating the information manipulation and reasoning capacity in humans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. We implement the long-term memory in our system with two external databases: (i) <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">recall memory</span> stores the complete conversation log in raw text form, following  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and (ii) <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contextual memory</span> is a hierarchical multimodal database storing information in the form of semantic concepts associated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> with each other through shared context from the corresponding conversation snapshot.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Stage-3: Retrieve</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Humans.</span> In cognitive science, the encoding specificity principle <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> proposed that memory stores information with its context; therefore, retrieval is most effective when the cues present at the time of retrieval match those present at the time of storage. Motivated by this principle, we also store concepts associated with their context in our contextual memory for effective context-driven retrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUGUSTUS</span>. While retrieving information from the contextual memory, given a user query, we perform a search over the stored semantic concepts represented with their associated context in the embedding space. Next, we retrieve the information associated with the corresponding concepts for the final response.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span><span id="algorithm1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Co</span>ntextual-<span id="algorithm1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pe</span>rsonalized (<span id="algorithm1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoPe</span>) Search</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.10" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.10.1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            <span id="algorithm1.10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function</span> <em id="algorithm1.10.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_typewriter">cope_search(<em id="algorithm1.10.1.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_serif ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="query,topk,\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">query,topk,\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math></em>)</em><span id="algorithm1.10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.10.2" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            get contextual tree from memory <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CT\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CT\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algorithm1.10.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">get_CT</span> (<math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>)
</div>
